00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.200 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  in our morning rounds a surprising new  study finds first-time mothers may be  able to safely shorten their pregnancies  to avoid complications the study in the  New England Journal of Medicine found  inducing labor at 39 weeks resulted in  fewer c-sections compared to waiting for  labor to begin naturally at 40 weeks  this contracts contradicts a long-held  belief that inducing labor leads to more  complications including c-sections a  typical pregnancy as we mentioned is 40  weeks researchers also found inducing  labor did not increase breathing  problems in babies and that lowered  mothers risk of high blood pressure  disorders our doctor Tara nurullah joins  us at the table Tara I was quite  surprised by reading this one expert  said this could be a game-changer is  this going to be the new normal going  forward well I think it's going to be an  option for sure and as you mentioned in  the past if a woman went to her OB and  said you know what can I have my labor  induced early the answer would have been  for most of them no let's wait until you  spontaneously go into labor because  there really was this belief that it  might increase these section rates and  increase complications to the baby and  the mom and so the fact though is that  about one out of four women end up  having an induction of Labor so  researchers really wanted to know what  are the consequences to the mom and the  baby  so they took two groups of women these  were low-risk women first-time moms and  one group they induced early at 39 weeks  the other group they allowed to progress  as they normally would to spontaneous  delivery and what they in fact found was  surprising that the induced group at 39  weeks had lower c-section rates had  lower rates of hypertensive  complications lower rates of postpartum  infection less labor pain and when they  looked at the babies because that's  important too there was really no  difference in terms of negative outcomes  or consequences to the babies really  like that less labor pain part but but  what is the magic number of 39 so 39  weeks is essentially considered  full-term and so after that point really  the benefit of having the baby and  longer is really not considered  significant and the lowest fetal  mortality is at 39 weeks so that's why  that really was chosen as the number and  we've seen more and more women have  c-sections in this country whether it's  or their doctor or elective or their  doctors telling them that they need to  because the baby's in distress could we  actually see that number lower and what  is doctors reaction to this we could and  right now really it's about 30% the  c-section rate that's very high it  should be almost half of that and you  know the issue about why c-sections  might be lower in the early induced  group we think could be a couple things  that are going on one the longer the  babies in the fetal size is bigger that  could lead to more c-sections also the  placenta may not work as well and that  makes it harder for the baby the fetus  to tolerate labor and then the longer  you're pregnant the more risk the mom is  for maternal medical complications and  also OB complications so certainly you  know this it makes sense that you may  have a lower c-section rate if you  deliver somebody earlier and c-sections  come with issues it is a surgery there's  risks of bleeding there's a blood clot  infection scarring to the uterus so that  in your next pregnancy  you put centum i implant in an abnormal  location injury to the bowel and the  bladder hysterectomy and list goes on  and on yeah not to mention that you have  to stay in the hospital longer so if  you're pregnant right now you might want  to call your doctor yeah certainly it's  worth a discussion I think that's what  ACOG and the Society for maternal fetal  medicine are saying that look based on  this study which they reviewed it's a  reasonable option for low-risk women to  discuss this with their dog  and have really a patient-centered  approach of shared decision-making  always good to have you here dr. Tao  Narula thank you very much

